# .lace performance rule

When writing or modifying code related to `.lace` loading, saving, serialization, mapping, or large diagram handling, always follow these rules.

## 1. Main objective

This application loads and saves potentially large `.lace` files with large descriptors and embedded resources.

Performance-sensitive code must optimize for:
- lower load time
- lower save time where reasonable
- lower peak memory usage
- fewer unnecessary allocations
- fewer unnecessary copies
- predictable behavior on standard desktop hardware

Do not write obviously wasteful code in hot paths.

## 2. Performance-sensitive areas

Treat the following as performance-sensitive by default:
- `.lace` archive opening
- ZIP entry scanning
- descriptor decoding
- protobuf parsing
- legacy XML compatibility loading
- mapping from persistence/serialization model to domain model
- writing descriptor and resources
- reloading after save
- loading large collections/elements inside diagrams

Be especially careful in these areas.

## 3. Avoid unnecessary intermediate structures

Do not create extra large intermediate representations unless clearly justified.

Avoid patterns such as:
- reading a large stream fully into a byte array when direct parsing is possible
- converting large structures back and forth unnecessarily
- building multiple equivalent object graphs
- copying large collections only to immediately remap them again
- constructing large strings for debug logging

Prefer streaming/direct parsing when compatible with architecture and code clarity.

## 4. Avoid duplicate graph construction

When decoding a large descriptor:
- avoid parsing into one large temporary model and then rebuilding an almost identical second model if a more direct mapping is possible
- keep mapping boundaries explicit, but do not tolerate obviously redundant full-graph duplication

If a duplication is required for architectural cleanliness, keep it justified and localized.

## 5. Memory awareness matters

Assume typical users may have limited RAM.

Therefore:
- avoid full in-memory duplication of large descriptor payloads
- release references promptly by keeping scopes tight
- prefer local variables with narrow lifetime
- avoid caching large persistence payloads without a strong reason
- be cautious with debug-only retained structures

Do not keep large temporary data around longer than necessary.

## 6. Logging must not hurt performance

In performance-sensitive paths:
- avoid expensive string concatenation for debug logging unless guarded appropriately
- do not log full descriptor contents
- keep logs concise
- prefer structured, phase-level logs such as timing and format selection

Logs should help diagnose hot paths without becoming a new cost center.

## 7. No accidental quadratic behavior

Be alert to accidental quadratic complexity in:
- repeated list scanning
- repeated map rebuilding
- repeated resource lookup
- repeated archive entry search
- repeated conversion of the same data

If the code repeatedly searches the same archive/resource/collection in a hot path, introduce a clearer and more efficient access strategy.

## 8. Performance instrumentation is encouraged

For hot paths, preserve or add lightweight timing instrumentation where appropriate, especially around:
- archive open
- entry discovery
- descriptor parse/decode
- mapping to domain
- resource load
- save completion

Instrumentation must be:
- low-noise
- debug-oriented
- cheap enough for normal development
- easy to remove or keep behind existing logging conventions

Do not add noisy ad hoc prints.

## 9. Choose clarity first, but not waste

Do not sacrifice maintainability for meaningless micro-optimization.
However, do remove obvious waste.

Good trade-off:
- clear architecture
- explicit mapping
- no giant unnecessary copies
- no obviously redundant full transformations

Bad trade-off:
- unreadable code for tiny gains
- or clean-looking code that is wildly wasteful on 300 MB descriptors

## 10. UI responsiveness matters

In a desktop JavaFX application, heavy `.lace` operations must not accidentally degrade UI responsiveness.

When touching code near heavy file operations:
- be conscious of which layer triggers the work
- keep long-running operations out of inappropriate UI-bound code paths
- do not introduce extra UI-thread blocking work casually
- preserve clear boundaries between UI orchestration and heavy persistence operations

Do not move expensive persistence work into controllers or rendering code.

## 11. Resource handling must be efficient

For image/resource persistence:
- avoid loading resources repeatedly when one controlled pass is enough
- avoid repeated archive scans for the same resource
- keep resource-reference lookup coherent and efficient
- avoid unnecessary transcoding/conversion unless explicitly required

## 12. Keep save behavior efficient too

Do not focus only on loading.
Saving can also become expensive.

When saving:
- avoid building redundant temporary archives in memory unless required
- avoid serializing equivalent data multiple times
- write only what is needed by the format contract
- keep resource enumeration and descriptor generation coherent and efficient

## 13. Benchmarks and measurement

When touching major hot paths, prefer changes that can be validated by repeatable measurement.

Useful measurement targets include:
- descriptor decode time
- full open time
- full save time
- save then reopen time
- memory-sensitive scenarios for large descriptors

Do not add brittle performance assertions to ordinary CI tests unless the repository already has a robust framework for that.

## 14. Keep debug-only features out of the hot path

Optional readable exports or debug dumps must not contaminate the normal load/save hot path.

If a debug-readable descriptor exists:
- it must be opt-in
- it must not be generated by default during normal saves
- it must not be parsed during normal loads unless explicitly requested

## 15. Refactor hot paths when touching them

When modifying a known hot path:
- inspect whether the touched code introduces new copies, scans, or mappings
- simplify the path if possible
- prefer reusable focused helpers over repeated ad hoc logic
- improve naming so hot-path responsibilities remain understandable

Do not leave obvious performance debt untouched when the area is already being changed.

## 16. Output expectations

Generated or modified code in performance-sensitive areas must:
- remain readable
- remain testable
- avoid obvious waste
- respect large-file desktop constraints
- preserve architecture while improving efficiency

If the existing codebase contains weaker patterns, improve the touched area toward this standard instead of copying them.